---
import LoopBand from '../LoopBand.astro';
import { cta, launchPlan } from '../../lib/site';
---

<!--
  LAYOUT. One measured column, then a full-container band. The previous version
  was `grid items-center lg:grid-cols-[7fr_5fr]` with a cropped screenshot in the
  narrow column: `items-center` centred a short figure against a much taller text
  column, so the fold rendered as a tall block of type beside a small panel
  floating in a dead band of its own. Reviewer's words: too much margin, not
  centred. There is no side column now, so there is nothing to mis-centre.

  WHAT THE FOLD ARGUES. It used to argue one narrow claim (a confirmed quote
  keeps its price) in the largest slot on the page. That is a good claim and it
  is the wrong one to lead with: a reader who cannot yet say what the product
  IS has no use for a guarantee about its edge case. The fold now names the
  whole chain and draws it, because the manual work being replaced is the chain,
  not the quote.
-->
<section class="container-page pt-10 pb-section-tight sm:pt-14 lg:pt-20">
	<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl text-center">
	<!-- The audience line names restaurants on purpose. Until 2026-08-30 it said
	     "caterers and meal-prep kitchens" while Who this is for, 2,000px down,
	     said "a catering-only kitchen or a restaurant too" (RC-44). A restaurant
	     owner reading the fold was told this is not for them and then told it
	     is. The scope is the same as RC-44: the restaurant work that runs by
	     event, menu, date and guest count, so "restaurants that cater", never
	     "restaurants". -->
	<p class="anim-enter text-sm font-semibold text-amber-deep">
		Back-of-house software for independent caterers, meal-prep kitchens, and restaurants that cater
	</p>

	<h1
		class="anim-enter mx-auto mt-4 max-w-[22ch] text-display font-semibold sm:mt-5"
		style="--enter-delay: 70ms"
	>
		Cost it, buy it, prep it, pack it. Enter the numbers once.
	</h1>

	<!-- MOVED HERE 2026-09-06, from the "See it run" h2 further down the page.
	     The heading above names the chain in the abstract; this names the job the
	     reader actually has on the calendar, so the fold now says what CostCook
	     does AND on what. It is the film's promise, and it is the third of the
	     seven headings in the documented heading-only scan
	     (docs/demo-video-rebuild-2026-08-21.md), which is why it reads as a claim
	     and not as a caption.

	     COST, AND WHO PAID IT. This line adds 100px. On its own that put the
	     pricing figure at 913.9px against the 844px fold at 320x844, and
	     verify-homepage.mjs failed on it: that suite asserts the proof starts
	     inside the first viewport on phones, which is the whole scroll cue.
	     Rather than shave margins to clear the number, both fold paragraphs step
	     from text-lede to text-base below sm, TOGETHER so their relative weight
	     is unchanged, and the section's top padding drops to pt-10. Measured
	     after: 795px at 320, 671px at 390 (it was 712px at 390 before this line
	     existed, so the fold is tighter than it looks here). Re-measure with
	     `npm run verify:homepage` before shipping any edit to these two
	     paragraphs; the 320px fold is the binding constraint, not 390px.
	     story: docs/stories/homepage-fold-promise.story.md -->
	<p
		class="anim-enter mx-auto mt-4 max-w-[54ch] text-base font-medium text-ink sm:mt-6 sm:text-lede"
		style="--enter-delay: 110ms"
	>
		Follow one 180-guest wedding from quote to shelf. Then three events in one run.
	</p>

	<!-- The lede does NOT re-list the chain: the band below draws it, and saying
	     it twice pushed the drawing under the fold on a phone. It says why the
	     chain matters instead. -->
	<p
		class="anim-enter mx-auto mt-3 max-w-[54ch] text-base text-ink-soft sm:mt-4 sm:text-lede"
		style="--enter-delay: 150ms"
	>
		A catering order is one job pretending to be six. Enter the menu and the head count
		once. The same numbers run to the pack list and back.
	</p>

	<div class="anim-enter mt-6 sm:mt-8" style="--enter-delay: 230ms">
		<a
			href={cta.href}
			target={cta.target}
			rel={cta.rel}
			aria-label={cta.ariaLabel}
			class="btn-primary"
		>
			{cta.label}
		</a>
		<!-- The interpolations sit inline: a newline before {expr} is collapsed
		     away by the compiler, which is how this line shipped reading
		     "then$49/month" for one build. -->
		<p class="mx-auto mt-3 max-w-[52ch] text-sm text-ink-soft sm:mt-4">
			<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">{launchPlan.trialDays} days free</strong>, then {launchPlan.displayPrice} per kitchen workspace. Card up front, charged $0 today. Nothing to install.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-3 text-sm sm:mt-4">
			<a href="#demo" class="btn-quiet"
				>Watch the 2:53 product tour <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a
			>
		</p>
	</div>

	</div>

	<!-- THE PRODUCT, ON THE FIRST SCREEN. Added 2026-08-29. Until now the first
	     pixel of the app was 2,600px down at 1440 and 4,500px down on a phone.
	     The B2B pages that convert put a real interface in the hero, and NN/g's
	     fold guidance says the first screen has to prove there is something
	     worth scrolling to. This is one straight-on crop of the pricing panel,
	     cut from public/demo.mp4 at 0:32 (RC-48), so a reader who scrolls to the
	     film sees the same order, and it sits low enough to straddle the fold at
	     both widths, which is the scroll cue.

	     NOT A PERSPECTIVE MOCKUP, NOT A DEVICE FRAME. It is paper: a ticket rule,
	     a mono label, the panel. Below sm it is the Next step box alone, because
	     the whole panel at 390px renders its small text at 6px.

	     ALT TEXT IS A CLAIM. Both alts read off the crop word for word; if the
	     crop moves, they move. story: docs/stories/homepage-visual-guidance.story.md -->
	<figure class="anim-enter mx-auto mt-6 max-w-3xl sm:mt-10" style="--enter-delay: 300ms">
		<!-- Considered Composite; not used because this is one fixed, three-step
		     explanation, not a reusable tree of content nodes. -->
		<div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-x-5 gap-y-1">
			<p class="font-mono text-eyebrow font-semibold tracking-wide text-amber-deep">
				QUOTE CHECK · DRAFT ORDER
			</p>
			<p class="font-mono text-xs font-semibold tracking-wide text-ink-soft">
				180 guests × $68 = $12,240 revenue
			</p>
		</div>
		<ol
			class="mt-4 grid gap-3 border-y border-hairline py-3 text-left sm:grid-cols-3 sm:gap-0"
			aria-label="How the quote check works"
		>
			<li class="flex gap-3 sm:px-4 sm:first:pl-0">
				<span class="font-mono text-xs font-semibold text-amber-deep">01</span>
				<div>
					<strong class="block text-sm font-semibold text-ink">Set the event</strong>
					<span class="block text-sm text-ink-soft">Guests × price per head</span>
				</div>
			</li>
			<li class="flex gap-3 border-t border-hairline pt-3 sm:border-l sm:border-t-0 sm:px-4 sm:pt-0">
				<span class="font-mono text-xs font-semibold text-amber-deep">02</span>
				<div>
					<strong class="block text-sm font-semibold text-ink">Check the food</strong>
					<span class="block text-sm text-ink-soft">$26.98 food per guest</span>
				</div>
			</li>
			<li class="flex gap-3 border-t border-hairline pt-3 sm:border-l sm:border-t-0 sm:px-4 sm:last:pr-0 sm:pt-0">
				<span class="font-mono text-xs font-semibold text-amber-deep">03</span>
				<div>
					<strong class="block text-sm font-semibold text-ink">Decide before sending</strong>
					<span class="block text-sm text-ink-soft">Charge at least $89.94 / guest</span>
				</div>
			</li>
		</ol>
		<div class="mt-3 border border-hairline bg-paper p-1 shadow-ticket sm:p-2">
			<img
				src="/proof/hero-pricing-mobile.png"
				alt="The Next step box on an order's pricing panel: charge at least $89.94 per guest to meet the 30 percent target."
				width={616}
				height={174}
				fetchpriority="high"
				decoding="async"
				class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-[308px] sm:hidden"
			/>
			<img
				src="/proof/hero-pricing.png"
				alt="An order's pricing panel. Live totals: revenue $12,240.00, theoretical food cost $4,856.55, food cost per guest $26.98. Food-cost target: 39.7 percent food cost, marked critical, 9.7 percentage points over target. Next step: charge at least $89.94 per guest to meet the 30 percent target."
				width={1284}
				height={548}
				fetchpriority="high"
				decoding="async"
				style="width: 642px; max-width: 100%"
				class="mx-auto hidden h-auto sm:block"
			/>
		</div>
		<figcaption class="mt-3 max-w-[64ch] text-sm text-ink-soft">
			The panel checks the price you entered against the food the order will require. At
			$68 a head, this event is at 39.7% food cost against a 30% target, so CostCook names
			the price that would meet it before the quote goes out.
		</figcaption>
	</figure>

	<div class="anim-enter" style="--enter-delay: 300ms">
		<LoopBand />
	</div>
</section>
